  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "How the French sound",
   "texto": [
    "Three ideas save you a lot of mistakes. **Uno:** many final consonants do not pronounce, but there are exceptions and it is appropriate to learn them with each word: in **Paris**, **salut** and **vous** the final consonant is mute.",
    "**Dos:** several letters together make one sound: **ou** = u (vous), **oi** = uá (moi), **au** and **eau** = o, **ai** = e, **ch** = sh (enchanté), **eu** = one e with lips of or (monsieur).",
    "**Tres:** some sequences with **n** or **m** are nasal when the consonant is not followed by another vocal: **bon**, **enchanté**, **bien**. Compare **bon** / **bonne**: in the second the vocal is oral and the consonant is heard."
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "Sounds we don't have.",
   "texto": [
    "The **u** alone (you, salut): put your lips to say «u» and say «i».",
    "**j** of **je** and **bonjour** sounds like a «sh» with voice (/ ∞ /). The **g** also sounds like this to **e**, **i** or **y** (**girafe**); to **a**, **o** or **u** it usually sounds like the «cat» (**gare**) g.",
    "The **r** is done at the bottom of the throat.",
    "The **h** never sounds.",
    "The final **e** is usually mute in words like **madame** («ma-dam»); it can sound like a short vocal in grammatical words like **je** and **ne**, especially when speaking carefully."
   ]
  },
